{
  "_readme": [
    "Two lists feed the Showcase Radar.",
    "",
    "`showcases` — events with a DATE SOMEONE ANNOUNCED. These are the only ones",
    "that fire countdown alerts, because an alert is a promise that the thing is",
    "really happening on that day. Add one the moment a date goes public.",
    "",
    "`recurring` — the annual rhythm. Nobody has announced Nintendo's September",
    "Direct yet, but there is always one, so the board can still say 'expected",
    "mid Sep' and you can plan around it. These roll over on their own every",
    "year and are always drawn with a ~ so nobody mistakes them for a real date.",
    "",
    "A `showcases` entry supersedes any `recurring` entry sharing its `series`",
    "in the same month — so when a date is announced, the estimate disappears",
    "instead of sitting next to it. This works for dates that have ALREADY",
    "PASSED too, and that is the point: once September's State of Play has",
    "aired, adding it here is what stops the board predicting another one.",
    "",
    "Rule shapes: {month|months, day} for a fixed date, or {month|months, week,",
    "weekday} for 'second Thursday' (weekday 0=Sun..6=Sat, week -1 = last).",
    "`precision` says how loudly to state it: day | week | month.",
    "`spanDays` is how many days it runs, counting the first: CES's {day 6,",
    "spanDays 4} is 6-9 Jan. A run stays on the board until its LAST day.",
    "",
    "`category` picks the emoji and where it lands: gaming | tech | mobile | ai,",
    "plus `sale` for storefront sales, which get their own board section because",
    "what you need from a sale is the deadline, not the start time. Sale dates",
    "below are the historical pattern, not announcements - Valve and co. confirm",
    "roughly a week out, which is why they all live under `recurring`. When a",
    "store does announce, add it to `showcases` with the real dates and it will",
    "start firing countdown alerts."
  ],

